+    <section class="journal" id="journal" aria-labelledby="journal-title">
+      <h2 id="journal-title">Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before the beans reach our shop, usually with a long email thread and a
+          sample tin on the cupping table. We buy directly from small farms and cooperatives in Colombia, Ethiopia,
+          Guatemala, and Sumatra, and we only commit to a harvest after cupping at least three separate lots. We score
+          each one blind, looking for sweetness, clean acidity, and a finish that doesn't fade the moment the cup cools.
+        </p>
+        <p>Relationships matter more than any single score. Many of our partners have supplied us for six years or more,
+          and we pay well above the Fairtrade minimum, agreeing on the price before the harvest so the farmers can plan
+          their year. Where we can, we pre-finance part of the crop, which helps producers cover labor and
+          processing costs during the lean months. Each lot arrives with its altitude, variety, and processing method
+          recorded, and we print the farm name on the bag.</p>
+        <p>Once the green coffee lands, it rests for a few weeks before we roast. We run small test batches first,
+          adjusting heat and airflow until the profile brings out what we tasted at origin rather than hiding it behind
+          roast flavors. Every production batch is cupped again the next morning, and anything that doesn't match the
+          sample gets set aside. We roast to order twice a week, so what you drink is almost always less than ten days
+          off the roaster.</p>
+        <p>Sustainability is part of the sourcing process, not an afterthought. We favor shade-grown and
+          organically managed farms, ship green coffee in reusable jute sacks, and send spent chaff and burlap to local
+          gardeners as mulch. Each year one of us travels to origin to walk the farms, taste the new crop, and listen to
+          what our partners need next season. Those trips shape what ends up on our menu.</p>
+      </div>
